r_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dr-jts","html_url":"https://github.com/dr-jts","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dr-jts/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dr-jts/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dr-jts/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dr-jts/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dr-jts/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dr-jts/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dr-jts/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dr-jts/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dr-jts/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-02-11T20:51:35Z","updated_at":"2026-02-11T20:51:35Z","body":"> With JTS OverlayNGRobust I get a MultiLineString result.\n\nYes, that's good news actually. It shows that the basic OverlayNG code catches the robustness failure, and falls back to using snapping to produce an answer.  The snapping collapses the geometry to lines.\n\nIt would be nice if it was possible to determine a polygonal result (like the old Overlay does).  But this is quite a bit less egregious than other reported OverlayNG failures, so it's lower in priority to try and fix.\n\n","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/libgeos/geos/issues/comments/3887133531/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null}]